WC.source = {"name":"GRFP_Autonomous_Catalyst_CoDesign_Refined.pdf","sha256":"2fb4c1e53baf567a5a2234362ff1984b1cc00e7b35d3b767a028ee6282cd53fa","prose_sentences":61,"notes":"GRFP_Catalyst_Refinement_Notes.html"};
WC.paragraphs = [{"id":"P01","page":1,"title":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"A catalyst is not necessarily the material that enters the reactor. Song et al. linked inherited precursor architecture to n-propanol selectivity under one activation protocol [1]. In a different reactor, Li et al. obtained Cu adparticles under CO reduction, but grain-boundary-rich surfaces under nitrogen that later restructured during CO operation [2]. Does activation overwrite precursor structure, or can both histories shape the working catalyst? Building on robotic gas-fed testing [3], I will develop a self-driving laboratory that learns these relationships while choosing what to make, measure, or repeat. CO reduction is the model reaction; transfer to CO2 reduction is not assumed."},{"id":"P02","page":1,"title":"The two hypotheses","text":"Activation-created features do not uniformly erase precursor architecture: I predict gas-dependent retention of structural and product contrasts. Separately, adaptive characterization and replication will reach a specified precision on the synthesis–activation interaction curve at lower resource cost, without worse validation error or calibration. The two hypotheses will be tested independently."},{"id":"P03","page":1,"title":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"I will robotically spray copper nitrate on carbon paper and apply Joule heating [1], recording calibrated temperature traces. A pilot will cross two dwell times, at fixed peak temperature and cooling, with CO or N2 activation. Total flow and the current–time program will be shared; all electrodes then enter identical CO-fed membrane-electrode-assembly (MEA) tests. Structural checks will assess comparable reduction endpoints; equal charge alone will not establish equal conversion. Independent coating/heating runs will be replicated, with day and channel balanced. Pilot variance and a predeclared meaningful interaction will determine confirmatory replication."},{"id":"P04","page":1,"title":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Diffraction and microscopy before activation, at its endpoint, and after operation will track oxide fractions, coherent-domain dimensions and particle connectivity. Blinded transmission electron microscopy (TEM) will assess selected surface and grain/subgrain structures; diffraction width alone cannot identify boundaries. Air-free transfer will be checked against deliberate exposure [4]. Destructive or perturbing measurements use identically prepared companions, with mismatch estimated experimentally. Heated-support controls, loading, ICP-OES contamination checks and wetting/CO-access tests constrain alternative pathways [5]. A cooling-history comparison, accepted only with matched oxide fractions, will challenge the architectural interpretation. Transport can mediate a structural effect rather than invalidate it."},{"id":"P05","page":1,"title":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"The primary outcome is late-window n-propanol partial current density at fixed total current in a two-hour screen; an early window tests evolution. Calibrated gas chromatography, liquid collection and quantitative nuclear magnetic resonance will check volatile-product recovery, collection delay, outlet flow and crossover [1,8]. Activation products are collected separately. Product ratios remain diagnostic; absolute production, charge balance and voltage remain reported. Selected 50-hour tests assess persistence, not industrial lifetime. The decisive comparison tests whether the precursor contrast changes with activation, then validates a predicted structure–performance response in a new preparation. A processing interaction alone cannot establish a specific active-site mechanism."},{"id":"P06","page":1,"title":"Interpreting possible results","text":"Gas-dependent retention with a replicated product interaction supports co-design. Equivalent structural and product contrasts across activation gases argue against that interaction within predeclared meaningful bounds. Retained structure with convergent products limits its relevance to the measured output; imprecise results remain unresolved."},{"id":"P07","page":2,"title":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"After assay validation, I will expand dwell time and activation-gas composition within the tested window. Let θ(t) be the CO-versus-N2 effect on late-window output at dwell t, minus that effect at a reference dwell. This interaction curve is the common target for decisions and scoring. Adaptive characterization and replication exist [6,7]; here the challenge is delayed, destructive assays on imperfectly matched companions. A hierarchical joint model will link processing, structure and performance, including preparation/reactor variation and measurement error. Pretest companion data can inform predictions without proving mediation; post-test data support interpretation."},{"id":"P08","page":2,"title":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"The controller selects a new preparation–activation pair, independent repeat or validated structural assay by expected reduction in integrated posterior variance of θ(t) per resource cost, using a fixed dwell grid and weights. Cost includes specimen preparation, instrument occupancy, analysis and delay; expensive assays must earn their use. A scheduler executes robotic coating/heating, cassette handling, gas switching and testing; delayed results update subsequent batches. Calibrations and safety checks are mandatory. Equipment faults invalidate measurements; reproducible material failures remain feasibility outcomes."},{"id":"P09","page":2,"title":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"I will collect a separate, policy-independent replicated grid with paired structural/product data, failures and costs. Replay reveals only acquired measurements; independent validation preparations remain hidden. A space-filling design and an adaptive synthesis–activation search with fixed characterization/replication share models, candidates, starting data, controls and budgets. The primary score is cost to the predeclared integrated-variance threshold for θ(t), subject to validation error and calibration limits on that same curve. Prospective campaigns interleaved across days test physical performance beyond replay. Condition and preparation holdouts distinguish transfer from reproducibility. Removing structural inputs tests their value; unresolved comparisons are not wins."},{"id":"P10","page":2,"title":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"My Yushin-group nanomaterials synthesis and robotic coating, electrochemistry and ICP-OES work at Lila Sciences prepare me for this project. Year 1 validates precursor reproducibility, recovery, throughput and interfaces; Year 2 closes the physical loop and builds the reference library; Year 3 tests new batches and persistence. I will seek a host with existing gas-fed testing and shared microscopy/NMR, extending one station before parallelizing. Unattended CO operation requires professionally reviewed interlocks and commissioning. Operando beamtime is optional."},{"id":"P11","page":2,"title":"Broader Impacts","text":"This project will support electrified chemical synthesis and reusable autonomous-research infrastructure. Subject to host agreements, I will release nonproprietary interfaces, fixtures, calibrations and datasets preserving failures and sample histories. I will develop “Build the Experiment, Not Just the Model,” a laptop-based undergraduate module that replays the campaign without robots or hazardous gases. Students will choose whether to make, measure or repeat under a resource budget. I will seek a course partner, pilot with approximately twenty students, and assess reasoning with a pre/post rubric on confounders, uncertainty and decision quality. An undergraduate mentee will help curate accessible documentation and exercises, linking energy-materials education to autonomous science."}];
WC.sentences = [{"id":"P01S01","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"A catalyst is not necessarily the material that enters the reactor.","plain":"The object you prepare can change before or while it does the chemistry.","why":"This establishes the distinction between the starting precursor and the working material.","watch":"It does not say every catalyst changes, or that every change improves performance."},{"id":"P01S02","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"Song et al. linked inherited precursor architecture to n-propanol selectivity under one activation protocol [1].","plain":"Some starting structural features persisted and were associated with which products formed in that prior study.","why":"This is the synthesis-history half of the motivation. The fixed activation leaves an interaction question.","watch":"Precursor inheritance is prior art, not your proposed discovery; association is not a unique active-site mechanism."},{"id":"P01S03","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"In a different reactor, Li et al. obtained Cu adparticles under CO reduction, but grain-boundary-rich surfaces under nitrogen that later restructured during CO operation [2].","plain":"The activation environment mattered in another experiment, and the nitrogen-treated surface continued changing after CO operation began.","why":"This motivates testing activation gas while preserving the distinction between activation endpoint and working state.","watch":"Different reactor. Nitrogen did not establish a permanently stable nanobump structure, and transfer to this MEA is not guaranteed."},{"id":"P01S04","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"Does activation overwrite precursor structure, or can both histories shape the working catalyst?","plain":"Does how you switch the material on erase how you made it—or does that history still matter?","why":"This is the materials question that organizes Aim 1.","watch":"These are not the only possible outcomes: partial retention, later erasure, and structure without a product effect are all possible."},{"id":"P01S05","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"Building on robotic gas-fed testing [3], I will develop a self-driving laboratory that learns these relationships while choosing what to make, measure, or repeat.","plain":"The lab will choose its next physical action, not just execute an unchanged list.","why":"This makes autonomous decision-making a research contribution, rather than bookkeeping.","watch":"Existing robotic gas-fed testing is a starting point. The complete host platform and integrations are not already confirmed."},{"id":"P01S06","pid":"P01","page":1,"section":"Opening: why the working catalyst matters","text":"CO reduction is the model reaction; transfer to CO2 reduction is not assumed.","plain":"The feed for the primary study is carbon monoxide, not carbon dioxide.","why":"This bounds the chemistry and the claims.","watch":"Upstream CO production, full carbon accounting, and direct CO2 performance are not established by this project."},{"id":"P02S01","pid":"P02","page":1,"section":"The two hypotheses","text":"Activation-created features do not uniformly erase precursor architecture: I predict gas-dependent retention of structural and product contrasts.","plain":"The difference between two preparations should depend on which gas is present during activation.","why":"It predicts an interaction that should be examined in both structure and products.","watch":"Neither a universal fine-grain advantage nor the direction of every response is promised."},{"id":"P02S02","pid":"P02","page":1,"section":"The two hypotheses","text":"Separately, adaptive characterization and replication will reach a specified precision on the synthesis–activation interaction curve at lower resource cost, without worse validation error or calibration.","plain":"Choosing intelligently when to measure or repeat should learn the same interaction more cheaply, without becoming confidently wrong.","why":"This is the independent autonomy hypothesis. Precision, error, and calibration must all be checked.","watch":"The numerical threshold, cost weights, and acceptable error limits are not specified in the two-page plan."},{"id":"P02S03","pid":"P02","page":1,"section":"The two hypotheses","text":"The two hypotheses will be tested independently.","plain":"Good catalyst science does not automatically mean the controller helped; a useful controller does not prove the catalyst mechanism.","why":"Each aim needs its own evidence.","watch":"A positive result in one column cannot rescue unsupported claims in the other."},{"id":"P03S01","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"I will robotically spray copper nitrate on carbon paper and apply Joule heating [1], recording calibrated temperature traces.","plain":"Prepare a coated electrode and use electrical heating to form the precursor, while measuring its actual thermal history.","why":"This supplies a reproducible, automatable synthesis intervention.","watch":"The refined draft does not commit to a particular temperature, loading, nozzle program, or dwell-time pair. Electrical power is not itself sample temperature."},{"id":"P03S02","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"A pilot will cross two dwell times, at fixed peak temperature and cooling, with CO or N2 activation.","plain":"Make all four combinations of two heating durations and two activation gases.","why":"Crossing the factors allows the effect of activation to be compared at each preparation setting.","watch":"Longer dwell is not automatically equivalent to larger grains or a particular oxide fraction; those are measurements."},{"id":"P03S03","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"Total flow and the current–time program will be shared; all electrodes then enter identical CO-fed membrane-electrode-assembly (MEA) tests.","plain":"Hold the imposed activation flow and electrical schedule fixed, change the activation gas, then test every electrode under CO.","why":"The primary manipulation is activation atmosphere; later operation is held common.","watch":"Same current program does not ensure the same cathode potential, internal environment, or reduction rate."},{"id":"P03S04","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"Structural checks will assess comparable reduction endpoints; equal charge alone will not establish equal conversion.","plain":"Check what material was actually produced instead of inferring it solely from the electrons passed.","why":"Charge can be partitioned among oxide reduction and other electrochemical processes.","watch":"Comparable endpoints are an experimental question. The proposal does not claim a universal conversion threshold."},{"id":"P03S05","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"Independent coating/heating runs will be replicated, with day and channel balanced.","plain":"Repeat the preparation process, not just the instrument reading, and distribute conditions across dates and reactor channels.","why":"This helps separate treatment differences from preparation scatter and equipment or day effects.","watch":"Several images or product injections from one electrode are not new independent preparations."},{"id":"P03S06","pid":"P03","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: synthesis and the crossed pilot","text":"Pilot variance and a predeclared meaningful interaction will determine confirmatory replication.","plain":"Use measured scatter and a scientifically relevant effect size to plan how many real repetitions are needed.","why":"The pilot estimates feasibility; the confirmatory comparison tests the hypothesis with adequate resolution.","watch":"No fixed sample number or guaranteed statistical power is supplied in the refined draft."},{"id":"P04S01","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Diffraction and microscopy before activation, at its endpoint, and after operation will track oxide fractions, coherent-domain dimensions and particle connectivity.","plain":"Look at the material at three stages, using measurements that describe different structural features.","why":"This asks where a preparation contrast survives or disappears.","watch":"Coherent diffraction domains, visible particles, and interfaces between grains are not interchangeable quantities."},{"id":"P04S02","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Blinded transmission electron microscopy (TEM) will assess selected surface and grain/subgrain structures; diffraction width alone cannot identify boundaries.","plain":"Use detailed microscopy on selected specimens without telling the analyst their treatment labels.","why":"It adds structural information that a bulk peak width cannot uniquely supply.","watch":"Selected fields must not be treated as a complete census of the whole electrode."},{"id":"P04S03","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Air-free transfer will be checked against deliberate exposure [4].","plain":"Compare a protected transfer with a deliberately exposed control to assess handling artifacts.","why":"The recovered sample must be a defensible proxy for what existed in the reactor.","watch":"A protected-transfer label is not proof that nothing changed; the control tests the handling method."},{"id":"P04S04","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Destructive or perturbing measurements use identically prepared companions, with mismatch estimated experimentally.","plain":"Use separately prepared sibling specimens when a measurement consumes or alters the material. Quantify how different siblings actually are.","why":"The decision model needs to know how informative a companion is about a performance electrode.","watch":"Identically prepared does not mean atomically identical, and a companion measurement is not a measurement of the exact tested electrode."},{"id":"P04S05","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Heated-support controls, loading, ICP-OES contamination checks and wetting/CO-access tests constrain alternative pathways [5].","plain":"Check whether substrate changes, catalyst amount, contamination, or reactant access could explain the observed response.","why":"A preparation changes an electrode, not just an idealized copper crystal.","watch":"These checks constrain explanations; they do not guarantee every possible cause is matched."},{"id":"P04S06","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"A cooling-history comparison, accepted only with matched oxide fractions, will challenge the architectural interpretation.","plain":"Use a second preparation route as a cross-check, but only call it phase-matched after measuring its oxide composition.","why":"If different routes support the same structure–response relationship, that strengthens the interpretation.","watch":"Matching oxide fractions does not guarantee matching wetting, strain, surface chemistry, or every other property."},{"id":"P04S07","pid":"P04","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: seeing structure and excluding look-alikes","text":"Transport can mediate a structural effect rather than invalidate it.","plain":"Structure might matter because it changes how CO reaches the reacting surface.","why":"Not every transport-related change is an unrelated artifact.","watch":"Separate a true intermediate pathway from an uncontrolled nuisance; do not automatically regress away every post-treatment variable."},{"id":"P05S01","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"The primary outcome is late-window n-propanol partial current density at fixed total current in a two-hour screen; an early window tests evolution.","plain":"Compare the fraction of electrical current going to propanol in a specified later collection interval, and also measure an earlier interval.","why":"The late-window output is the quantity the interaction curve will describe.","watch":"Exact early/late boundaries and total current are not supplied. Two hours is a screen, not a lifetime claim."},{"id":"P05S02","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"Calibrated gas chromatography, liquid collection and quantitative nuclear magnetic resonance will check volatile-product recovery, collection delay, outlet flow and crossover [1,8].","plain":"Measure products through a validated collection-and-analysis chain, including where material can be delayed or missed.","why":"The controller must learn chemistry, not an uncorrected collection artifact.","watch":"An autosampler is not the same as instantaneous NMR; the full analysis may be delayed."},{"id":"P05S03","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"Activation products are collected separately.","plain":"Do not mix products made while the catalyst is being activated with products from the common operating test.","why":"This preserves the definition of the response being compared.","watch":"A combined collection would confound formation history with the reported operating outcome."},{"id":"P05S04","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"Product ratios remain diagnostic; absolute production, charge balance and voltage remain reported.","plain":"Ratios can help interpret branching, but keep actual output and electrical accounting visible.","why":"A better ratio can occur even while useful production falls.","watch":"FE, partial current, product ratios, and energy use answer different questions."},{"id":"P05S05","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"Selected 50-hour tests assess persistence, not industrial lifetime.","plain":"Run a limited longer test to see whether a response lasts beyond screening.","why":"A useful initial working structure may continue changing.","watch":"The result is bounded to that duration and operating environment; it is not a commercial-lifetime forecast."},{"id":"P05S06","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"The decisive comparison tests whether the precursor contrast changes with activation, then validates a predicted structure–performance response in a new preparation.","plain":"Estimate a difference of differences, then test a prediction using material that was not used to form that explanation.","why":"New preparation evidence tests whether the learned relationship is reproducible.","watch":"A favorable point selected from the same data is not held-out validation."},{"id":"P05S07","pid":"P05","page":1,"section":"Aim 1: product accounting and the decisive comparison","text":"A processing interaction alone cannot establish a specific active-site mechanism.","plain":"Even a real interaction does not identify the exact atomic site responsible for propanol.","why":"This caps the causal claim at the level supported by the observations.","watch":"Microscopy plus a product curve is not automatically proof of one elementary reaction pathway."},{"id":"P06S01","pid":"P06","page":1,"section":"Interpreting possible results","text":"Gas-dependent retention with a replicated product interaction supports co-design.","plain":"When the preserved structure and the product contrast both depend reproducibly on activation gas, joint preparation–activation design gains support.","why":"The positive evidence links the material and performance comparisons.","watch":"Support is not proof of a unique atomic mechanism or a universal rule for all copper systems."},{"id":"P06S02","pid":"P06","page":1,"section":"Interpreting possible results","text":"Equivalent structural and product contrasts across activation gases argue against that interaction within predeclared meaningful bounds.","plain":"If the contrasts are sufficiently alike to rule out a meaningful difference, the interaction hypothesis is not supported in this window.","why":"This allows an informative negative result.","watch":"No significant difference is not automatically equivalence; uncertainty must be small enough for the declared bounds."},{"id":"P06S03","pid":"P06","page":1,"section":"Interpreting possible results","text":"Retained structure with convergent products limits its relevance to the measured output; imprecise results remain unresolved.","plain":"A structural difference may not affect this product under these conditions; noisy data may simply not settle the question.","why":"The project distinguishes irrelevant-to-this-output from unmeasured or inconclusive.","watch":"Do not convert either outcome into proof that structure never matters."},{"id":"P07S01","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"After assay validation, I will expand dwell time and activation-gas composition within the tested window.","plain":"Only after the measurement works, explore more preparation settings and feasible gas mixtures.","why":"The initial 2×2 is a pilot, not the entire eventual research space.","watch":"No expansion outside a validated operating domain is promised."},{"id":"P07S02","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"Let θ(t) be the CO-versus-N2 effect on late-window output at dwell t, minus that effect at a reference dwell.","plain":"First compare the two gases at each dwell. Then subtract the same gas effect at a chosen reference dwell.","why":"This isolates how the gas effect changes with preparation rather than confusing it with a constant gas benefit.","watch":"Here t means heating dwell, not time spent operating the catalyst. θ at the reference dwell is zero by definition."},{"id":"P07S03","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"This interaction curve is the common target for decisions and scoring.","plain":"The controller, hypothesis, and benchmark all refer to the same thing.","why":"The system cannot claim success by optimizing one quantity and evaluating another.","watch":"The best-yield recipe is not the primary autonomy target."},{"id":"P07S04","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"Adaptive characterization and replication exist [6,7]; here the challenge is delayed, destructive assays on imperfectly matched companions.","plain":"Choosing measurements or repetitions is prior art; this proposal tests those choices with difficult real specimens and delays.","why":"This states the bounded autonomous-science contribution.","watch":"It is not a verified first-ever claim about active learning or instrument selection."},{"id":"P07S05","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"A hierarchical joint model will link processing, structure and performance, including preparation/reactor variation and measurement error.","plain":"The model represents related levels of variation and relationships between measurements rather than treating every number as an independent exact datum.","why":"It provides the statistical bridge through which a structural assay can inform the product interaction.","watch":"The two-page plan does not specify a complete model architecture, priors, kernel, or identifiability proof."},{"id":"P07S06","pid":"P07","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: one learning target and a joint model","text":"Pretest companion data can inform predictions without proving mediation; post-test data support interpretation.","plain":"A companion measured before a performance outcome is known can inform a prediction. Later measurements help explain what happened.","why":"Prediction-time availability and causal interpretation are separate issues.","watch":"Using post-test information to claim a pretest prediction is leakage; predictive association is not causal mediation."},{"id":"P08S01","pid":"P08","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"The controller selects a new preparation–activation pair, independent repeat or validated structural assay by expected reduction in integrated posterior variance of θ(t) per resource cost, using a fixed dwell grid and weights.","plain":"For each possible action, estimate how much it would shrink uncertainty about the interaction curve, then divide by the declared cost.","why":"Make, measure, and repeat can be compared on one objective.","watch":"The grid, weights, uncertainty threshold, and cost weights must be fixed; the numerical values are not given in the draft."},{"id":"P08S02","pid":"P08","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"Cost includes specimen preparation, instrument occupancy, analysis and delay; expensive assays must earn their use.","plain":"Count the whole cost of getting an informative result, including companions and waiting.","why":"A nominally fast preparation can still have a slow or costly measurement loop.","watch":"Expensive does not mean useless; cheap does not mean informative."},{"id":"P08S03","pid":"P08","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"A scheduler executes robotic coating/heating, cassette handling, gas switching and testing; delayed results update subsequent batches.","plain":"Software turns the chosen action into physical laboratory jobs, and incorporates slow results when they arrive.","why":"This is the physical closed loop, not just offline optimization.","watch":"Not every facility instrument must be robotically controlled; manual interfaces and real delays must be documented."},{"id":"P08S04","pid":"P08","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"Calibrations and safety checks are mandatory.","plain":"The optimizer is not permitted to skip the controls that make the experiment valid and safe.","why":"Scientific validity and safety are constraints, not optional purchases.","watch":"This document is not an operational CO or heating safety protocol."},{"id":"P08S05","pid":"P08","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: acquisition and physical execution","text":"Equipment faults invalidate measurements; reproducible material failures remain feasibility outcomes.","plain":"A broken line is not a bad catalyst score; repeated delamination caused by a preparation is meaningful evidence about feasibility.","why":"The model needs distinct failure categories rather than a single missing-data bin.","watch":"Do not reward fragile recipes by deleting all their material failures."},{"id":"P09S01","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"I will collect a separate, policy-independent replicated grid with paired structural/product data, failures and costs.","plain":"Build the replay archive using a fixed collection plan, not only the conditions a favored adaptive method chose.","why":"This makes the archive provenance transparent and supports bounded policy comparisons.","watch":"The library costs real bench work and is not an unlimited map of all possible experiments."},{"id":"P09S02","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"Replay reveals only acquired measurements; independent validation preparations remain hidden.","plain":"A simulated policy receives only the measurements it has selected and paid for; reserved tests do not guide its choices.","why":"This prevents using future or unpaid information.","watch":"Replaying the same archive does not create new independent physical experiments."},{"id":"P09S03","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"A space-filling design and an adaptive synthesis–activation search with fixed characterization/replication share models, candidates, starting data, controls and budgets.","plain":"Compare against both a fixed coverage strategy and a capable adaptive baseline, under the same allowed domain and resources.","why":"Any advantage should come from action selection rather than a broader search or extra data.","watch":"A simple manual straw-man is not the stated baseline."},{"id":"P09S04","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"The primary score is cost to the predeclared integrated-variance threshold for θ(t), subject to validation error and calibration limits on that same curve.","plain":"Count how much it costs to become sufficiently precise, but only accept that precision if independent checks show appropriate accuracy and uncertainty.","why":"A narrow but wrong posterior fails.","watch":"The threshold and error/calibration limits are still design choices to set before confirmatory outcomes."},{"id":"P09S05","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"Prospective campaigns interleaved across days test physical performance beyond replay.","plain":"Run model-selected experiments in the real laboratory, spreading policy campaigns across days rather than giving each a different drift period.","why":"This tests execution, new material, and real delays that archive replay cannot fully establish.","watch":"One good campaign does not prove universal algorithmic superiority."},{"id":"P09S06","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"Condition and preparation holdouts distinguish transfer from reproducibility.","plain":"Test both new recipes and fresh preparations of known recipes, grouping related observations so they do not leak across the split.","why":"These are different generalization claims.","watch":"New images from a known electrode are not a new batch or a new preparation condition."},{"id":"P09S07","pid":"P09","page":2,"section":"Aim 2: replay, baselines and validation","text":"Removing structural inputs tests their value; unresolved comparisons are not wins.","plain":"Compare the model with and without structure information, and report uncertain policy comparisons as uncertain.","why":"This tests whether characterization actually contributed rather than assuming it helped.","watch":"The ablation must still use fair splits, costs, and comparable model evaluation."},{"id":"P10S01","pid":"P10","page":2,"section":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"My Yushin-group nanomaterials synthesis and robotic coating, electrochemistry and ICP-OES work at Lila Sciences prepare me for this project.","plain":"The proposal connects your stated experience to the material preparation, instrument integration, and analytical work needed.","why":"It explains why this is an extension of your preparation rather than an arbitrary topic.","watch":"This walkthrough preserves the statement; it does not add new experience or independently certify every employment detail."},{"id":"P10S02","pid":"P10","page":2,"section":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"Year 1 validates precursor reproducibility, recovery, throughput and interfaces; Year 2 closes the physical loop and builds the reference library; Year 3 tests new batches and persistence.","plain":"Validate the chemistry and measurements first, integrate the loop second, then test reproducibility, transfer, and longer operation.","why":"The stages prevent the whole project depending on a finished robot before any science can begin.","watch":"This is the proposed schedule, not a record of completed work."},{"id":"P10S03","pid":"P10","page":2,"section":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"I will seek a host with existing gas-fed testing and shared microscopy/NMR, extending one station before parallelizing.","plain":"The project assumes access will be established with a suitable host and grows from one functioning station.","why":"This bounds the hardware integration burden.","watch":"No named host, instrument booking, or collaboration is confirmed by this sentence."},{"id":"P10S04","pid":"P10","page":2,"section":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"Unattended CO operation requires professionally reviewed interlocks and commissioning.","plain":"Experts must review and commission the system before it runs unattended.","why":"Autonomy does not authorize experiments beyond approved operating constraints.","watch":"A browser demonstration is not equipment-control software or a safety approval."},{"id":"P10S05","pid":"P10","page":2,"section":"Preparation, feasibility and the three-year plan","text":"Operando beamtime is optional.","plain":"Measurements during operation at a beamline can strengthen selected interpretations but are not the sole basis of the core experiment.","why":"The project should retain a useful path if beamtime is unavailable.","watch":"Optional does not mean irrelevant; it limits which specific mechanistic claims can be made without it."},{"id":"P11S01","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"This project will support electrified chemical synthesis and reusable autonomous-research infrastructure.","plain":"The intended benefits extend beyond a single catalyst: methods and infrastructure can support future research.","why":"This opens the broader-impact section.","watch":"It is not a demonstrated climate benefit, industrial deployment, or commercial product."},{"id":"P11S02","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"Subject to host agreements, I will release nonproprietary interfaces, fixtures, calibrations and datasets preserving failures and sample histories.","plain":"Share reusable, documented components and complete records when agreements permit.","why":"Failures and provenance help others understand and reproduce a campaign.","watch":"No permission to disclose proprietary employer data is implied."},{"id":"P11S03","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"I will develop “Build the Experiment, Not Just the Model,” a laptop-based undergraduate module that replays the campaign without robots or hazardous gases.","plain":"Create a teaching exercise where students can practice experimental decisions using recorded data.","why":"It makes the reasoning accessible without the full laboratory infrastructure.","watch":"The module is proposed, not already deployed. This walkthrough uses synthetic examples, not the future experimental archive."},{"id":"P11S04","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"Students will choose whether to make, measure or repeat under a resource budget.","plain":"Learners must decide what evidence is worth collecting, not merely fit a supplied dataset.","why":"This mirrors the intellectual problem of Aim 2.","watch":"A budget game is a teaching exercise, not evidence that the research controller outperforms a baseline."},{"id":"P11S05","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"I will seek a course partner, pilot with approximately twenty students, and assess reasoning with a pre/post rubric on confounders, uncertainty and decision quality.","plain":"Arrange a partner course, try the module with about twenty students, and assess how their experimental reasoning changes.","why":"The impact plan includes a delivery mechanism and an assessment.","watch":"The partner is unconfirmed; twenty is a proposed scale, not an enrolled cohort or a power calculation."},{"id":"P11S06","pid":"P11","page":2,"section":"Broader Impacts","text":"An undergraduate mentee will help curate accessible documentation and exercises, linking energy-materials education to autonomous science.","plain":"Include an undergraduate in producing understandable, usable materials and learning the research workflow.","why":"This connects mentoring with the open education deliverable.","watch":"No particular student or recruitment commitment is established."}];
WC.refNotes = [{"id":"1","html":"<h2>1. What was checked and changed</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Feedback</th><th>Decision and qualification</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Binois reference</td><td>Corrected reference [7] to <em>Technometrics</em> <strong>61, 7–23 (2019)</strong>. The coauthor’s <a href=\"https://bobby.gramacy.com/papers/\">publication record</a> confirms the page range for <em>Replication or exploration? Sequential design for stochastic simulation experiments</em>. The previous 152–165 range was an error.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Precursor retention versus activation-driven reconstruction</td><td>The opening now contrasts Song’s precursor-inheritance result with Li’s gas-dependent activation result, then asks whether activation overwrites precursor structure or both histories shape the working material. In <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-018-07032-0\">Li et al.</a>, nitrogen reduction produced a grain-boundary-rich surface; bumps appeared during subsequent CO operation. This is not evidence for permanently preserved “nitrogen-created bumps,” nor proof of transfer to our MEA. The pilot now uses N2 instead of Ar to follow that precedent more directly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Common autonomy target</td><td>The hypothesis, decision rule, and primary benchmark all target the same synthesis–activation interaction curve. General performance prediction remains supporting evidence, rather than an interchangeable primary objective.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Replay-library provenance</td><td>The plan explicitly collects a separate, policy-independent replicated grid. Independent validation preparations are reserved. The archive is not retrospectively defined by the adaptive policy’s favored samples, and generating it is real experimental work.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Autonomy novelty</td><td><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34919429/\">SARA</a> already combines synthesis and characterization choices; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.03206\">Binois et al.</a> already treats replication versus exploration. The proposed extension evaluates such choices with delayed, destructive assays on imperfect companion electrodes. This is a bounded research contribution to test—not a claim to have invented active learning or verified a universal first.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Early versus persistent selectivity</td><td><a href=\"https://light.northwestern.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Advanced-Materials-2025-Song-Copper-Catalysts-Inherit-and-Retain-Precatalyst-Morphology-in-Extended-CO.pdf\">Song’s Figure 3</a> includes C3/C2 differences in the initial performance comparison, not solely during extended aging. The plan retains both early and late sampling; longer testing evaluates persistence separately. Absolute n-propanol output remains primary and product ratios remain diagnostic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PDF metadata</td><td>The PDF Subject field is empty; the DOCX subject and comments properties are also empty. This is editorial cleanup, not a separate NSF eligibility or originality requirement.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table><p class=\"small\">Scope: these edits use the reviewer summary pasted into the conversation, the actual revised proposal, and the sources linked here. The available 45-page review PDF contains the original review; the new “Revision review” tab and its line-edit table were not available in that file. The reported 19-issue tally was not independently reconstructed.</p>"},{"id":"2","html":"<h2>2. One target for hypothesis, decisions, and evaluation</h2><p>Let <em>m(t,a)</em> be the expected late-window n-propanol partial current density at heating dwell <em>t</em> and activation-gas composition <em>a</em>, under the shared operating protocol. The primary collection window, reference dwell, candidate domain, and scientifically meaningful differences must be fixed before confirmatory outcomes are examined.</p><div class=\"equation\">g(t) = m(t, CO) − m(t, N2)<br/>θ(t) = g(t) − g(t<sub>ref</sub>)</div><p><em>g(t)</em> is the activation effect at a preparation setting. Subtracting the effect at a reference dwell gives an interaction curve: an activation benefit that is constant across dwell times produces θ(t) = 0. This avoids mistaking a gas main effect for a synthesis–activation interaction. The initial 2×2 comparison is one contrast on that curve; it is not the entire eventual research domain.</p><p>Choose a fixed evaluation grid {t<sub>i</sub>} and nonnegative weights w<sub>i</sub> summing to one. Define the controller’s uncertainty target as:</p><div class=\"equation\">V(D) = Σ<sub>i</sub> w<sub>i</sub> Var[θ(t<sub>i</sub>) | D]<br/>U(action) = {V(D) − E[V(D ∪ new observations) | action]} / c(action)</div><p>The score is the expected reduction in that same integrated variance per declared resource cost. The joint model connects processing, structure, and performance, while retaining direct processing-to-performance effects. It explicitly represents preparation variation, reactor variation, assay noise, and companion mismatch. It must not force every processing effect to pass through a measured structural descriptor.</p><p>A characterization action informs θ only through a validated statistical relationship with the tested specimens. When the descriptor is unavailable, the model integrates over its uncertainty. If an assay carries little information about θ, the controller should learn not to buy it. This predictive relationship is not proof of causal mediation.</p><h3>How the policies will be scored</h3><p><strong>Primary endpoint:</strong> the accumulated cost required to reach V(D) ≤ τ², subject to predeclared validation-error and calibration limits on θ. All policies use the same grid, weights, threshold, and cost definition. A narrow but wrong posterior fails the validation requirement; lower estimated uncertainty alone is not a win.</p><p>The validation curve is estimated from reserved independent preparations, not assumed to be noiseless truth. Its sampling uncertainty and shared-reference covariance must be propagated. Check coverage and prediction error over multiple held-out contrasts and campaign blocks where feasible. Validation results are for scoring, not fed back into the controller being evaluated. Policies that fail to reach the target within budget remain reported as such.</p><p>Resource weights convert preparation, instrument occupancy, analysis, and delay into the declared cost measure. Report the uncombined totals and sensitivity to those weights as well. Human interventions, product-prediction error, and selected long-duration behavior remain important secondary outputs.</p>"},{"id":"3","html":"<h2>3. Where the replay data come from</h2><p><strong>Commissioning pilot:</strong> establish safe conditions, reproducibility, reduction-state checks, product recovery, measurement precision, and realistic action costs. Use it to define a bounded candidate domain, not to choose the benchmark that flatters a favored policy.</p><p><strong>Reference archive:</strong> collect a separate fixed grid with independent coating/heating preparations, both reference gases, additional feasible gas compositions as planned, and the structural/product measurements needed to replay the allowed actions. Record time stamps, failed runs, missing measurements, and actual costs. Where assays destroy a specimen, include the required companion preparation rather than silently treating the assay as free.</p><p><strong>Acquisition and validation split:</strong> reserve independent preparations before replay. Keep all images, time windows, assay repeats, and companions associated with each preparation in their assigned group. A policy can see an acquisition-pool measurement only after selecting and paying for its corresponding action; it cannot draw endlessly from the same electrode or reveal a destructive measurement that the archive never collected.</p><p>The archive is policy-independent—not generated by the adaptive controller or scored only at locations it chose. A fixed-grid policy may naturally be well matched to that domain; the purpose is a transparent finite-domain comparison, not a guarantee of neutral performance for every imaginable algorithm. State the coverage limitation and test new conditions prospectively. Repeated replay is not repeated physical synthesis.</p><p><strong>Prospective tests:</strong> interleave resource-matched policy campaigns across days or blocks, with separately tracked data available to each policy and shared compulsory controls. Test genuinely new preparations and selected withheld conditions. Report uncertainty appropriate to the actual number of physical campaigns. Building the archive and reserved validation set has a separately budgeted experimental cost; it is not claimed to require “no extra bench time.”</p>"},{"id":"4","html":"<h2>4. What the materials experiment can conclude</h2><p>The proposed comparison varies activation atmosphere at shared flow and imposed current–time history. Actual reduction endpoints still require independent checks: equal charge is not equal oxide conversion, and equal MEA current is not a guarantee of equal cathode potential. Nitrogen-versus-CO behavior from a different reactor motivates the intervention but does not establish its feasibility here.</p><p>Measure both activation endpoints and subsequent operating states. Retained precursor differences under one gas but convergence under the other would support gas-dependent structural inheritance; other patterns can reveal additive effects or later erasure. A product interaction is not automatically a grain-boundary mechanism. Wetting and transport may be alternatives or genuine mediators of a useful architecture effect, depending on the causal pathway.</p><p>Matched oxide fractions in a cooling-history control must be demonstrated within justified analytical limits. That control does not guarantee that every other property is matched. Where companions are needed, their consistency is an empirical quantity. Destructive or potentially perturbing measurements cannot be presumed harmless on the performance electrode.</p><p>A precise absence of an interaction can be informative even when both preparation and activation have main effects. Structural retention without a corresponding product difference bounds relevance to the measured output and operating window—not every possible catalytic property. Insufficient resolution remains an unresolved outcome.</p>"},{"id":"5","html":"<h2>5. What remains unchanged</h2><p>Physical robotic execution, prospective validation, independent preparation replication, separate product collection windows, absolute production, recovery checks, selected longer tests, and the distinction between equipment faults and reproducible material infeasibility all remain. Safety and calibration are mandatory and cannot be optimized away.</p><p>Host infrastructure, assay limits, reference-library size, resource weights, and the exact treatment/collection windows remain to be agreed with a prospective adviser. The hypothesis and workflow should be reproducible before the campaign expands. This refinement does not introduce a new chemistry, extra instrument family, or a promise of industrial lifetime.</p>"},{"id":"6","html":"<h2>6. Submission-shaped artifact</h2><p>The accompanying PDF contains two pages including eight references, uses 11-point Computer Modern-family body text and 12-point line spacing, keeps text inside one-inch blank margins, and retains separate <strong>Intellectual Merit</strong> and <strong>Broader Impacts</strong> headings with a blank line before the latter. The <a href=\"https://www.nsf.gov/funding/opportunities/grfp-nsf-graduate-research-fellowship-program/nsf26-526/solicitation\">official NSF 26-526 solicitation</a> remains the source of submission requirements. The PDF is the fixed-layout reference; the editable Word file may reflow with font substitution.</p><div class=\"footer\">The applicant should rewrite the final submission in their own voice and verify personal statements, access arrangements, safety approvals, outreach commitments, and host data-release conditions. These notes are for scientific development and are not part of the two-page research statement.</div>"}];
WC.glossaryBase = [{"term":"Activation","def":"The treatment that turns a prepared precursor into the state used for testing. Here the pilot changes its gas environment while sharing the imposed electrical schedule.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Active site","def":"A local region where a catalytic elementary step occurs. A measured product interaction does not uniquely identify its atomic structure.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Adparticle","def":"A small cluster attached to a larger surface. The proposal cites gas-dependent Cu adparticle formation as precedent in a different reactor, not a guaranteed outcome in this project.","src":"P01"},{"term":"Ablation","def":"A comparison in which a model component or information source is removed. Here structural inputs are removed to test whether they improve predictions.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Acquisition rule","def":"The rule scoring possible next experiments. Here it uses expected reduction in integrated uncertainty about θ divided by resource cost.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Activation gas","def":"CO or N₂ in the initial pilot. This is distinct from the common operating feed: all electrodes subsequently operate under CO.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Architecture","def":"The organization of particles, coherent domains, grains, and contacts. These are separate descriptors, not a single universally measurable number.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Assay","def":"A specified measurement procedure, including calibration and quality checks. A valid number requires a valid assay and recovery chain.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Autonomy","def":"The model’s decision changes the next executed physical experiment. Unattended execution of a fixed list alone does not establish this decision loop.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Baseline policy","def":"A comparison strategy given the same domain, starting data, models, controls, and resources. The draft includes a space-filling design and a restricted adaptive search.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Batch","def":"A group of preparations or scheduled experiments. Data from related batches, electrodes, and companions must retain their lineage. Exact grouping is an implementation choice.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Blinding","def":"Withholding treatment identities from an analyst to reduce interpretation bias. It does not, by itself, ensure representative sampling.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Calibration","def":"For an instrument: checking measurements against standards. For uncertainty: checking whether predicted uncertainty behaves reliably against independent evidence. These are distinct uses of the same word.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Carbon paper","def":"The supporting electrode substrate used for coating and Joule heating. It is not the CO gas reactant. Support changes are one possible explanation to check.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Cassette","def":"A proposed physical holder/interface enabling reproducible electrode handling and transfer. The detailed design is not given in the draft.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Catalyst","def":"A material that enables a reaction pathway. In this project its working structure may differ from its initially prepared state; it is not assumed physically unchanged during operation.","src":"P01"},{"term":"Cathode","def":"The electrode at which reduction occurs. The copper-based catalyst participates in the reduction side of this electrolyzer. The total cell voltage is not a direct cathode-potential measurement.","src":"P03"},{"term":"CO","def":"Carbon monoxide, the model reactant feed during every common performance test. It is also one of the two pilot activation gases.","src":"P01"},{"term":"CO₂","def":"Carbon dioxide. The proposal does not assume that a result for supplied CO automatically transfers to direct CO₂ reduction.","src":"P01"},{"term":"Coherent domain","def":"A region contributing coherently to diffraction. Its inferred dimension is not automatically the size of a visible particle or the density of grain boundaries.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Companion electrode","def":"A separately prepared sibling used when measurement would consume or perturb the performance electrode. Its mismatch is measured and included in the model.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Condition holdout","def":"A preparation/activation condition withheld from model training. It tests transfer to an unobserved recipe, rather than repetition of a known recipe.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Confounder","def":"A variable that can bias an inferred comparison. Do not classify every treatment-induced change this way: transport or wetting may instead be part of the effect being investigated.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Covariance","def":"How uncertainty in two quantities varies together. Interaction estimates share a reference dwell, so their errors are not automatically independent.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Crossover","def":"Product moving to another part of the cell, such as across the membrane, affecting where it must be collected and quantified.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Destructive assay","def":"A measurement that consumes the specimen or makes it unsuitable for the intended subsequent test. It carries both preparation cost and specimen limitations.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Dwell time","def":"Duration of the heating hold. In θ(t), t denotes this preparation variable—not elapsed electrolysis time.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Electrode loading","def":"Amount of catalyst associated with electrode area. A preparation difference must not be interpreted as architecture without accounting for differences in material amount.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Equivalence","def":"Evidence that a difference lies inside a predeclared meaningful tolerance. A wide interval including zero is not sufficient.","src":"P06"},{"term":"Faradaic efficiency","def":"The fraction of measured charge attributable to a particular product, given a valid product and charge accounting procedure. At fixed total current, it scales directly with that product’s partial current density.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Feasibility outcome","def":"A valid observation that a material or preparation cannot reliably survive or function under the specified conditions. It is not the same as an equipment fault invalidating a measurement.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Gas chromatography","def":"A calibrated analytical method for separating and quantifying appropriate components. It works with the liquid collection and quantitative NMR chain in this proposal.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Gaussian model","def":"A probabilistic representation using normal distributions. The teaching simulator uses a simple correlated Gaussian update; the actual draft commits only to a hierarchical joint model, not this exact implementation.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Grain boundary","def":"An interface between crystal regions with different orientations. Bulk diffraction width alone cannot establish boundary density.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Hierarchical model","def":"A model representing linked levels of variation, such as preparation, reactor, specimen, and measurement, instead of treating all observations as interchangeable independent data.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Holdout","def":"Data withheld from fitting or acquisition and used to evaluate predictions. Related images, assays, and companions must remain in the appropriate preparation group.","src":"P09"},{"term":"ICP-OES","def":"Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy: an elemental-analysis technique used here to check contamination and relevant material accounting. It does not tell you where an atom sat in the catalyst.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Integrated posterior variance","def":"A weighted sum of current variances of θ across a fixed dwell grid. It is the controller’s uncertainty target, not the expected product yield.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Interaction","def":"A change in the effect of one factor across levels of another. Here it is how the CO-versus-N₂ activation effect changes with heating dwell.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Ionomer","def":"An ion-containing polymer used in electrochemical electrode environments. Its amount and distribution can affect interfaces; the final recipe is not specified in the refined draft.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Joule heating","def":"Heating associated with electrical current through a resistive path. In this proposal the measured temperature history, not electrical power alone, records the thermal treatment.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Late window","def":"The specified later collection interval of the two-hour screen used for the primary response. Its exact boundaries are not yet supplied.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Leakage","def":"Information unavailable at decision time improperly entering a model or benchmark. Post-test data cannot be used to claim a pretest prediction of that same test.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Main effect","def":"A treatment’s effect considered separately from whether it changes across the other factor. A constant gas benefit cancels from θ.","src":"P07"},{"term":"MEA","def":"Membrane-electrode assembly: electrodes organized with an ion-conducting membrane. The refined plan does not specify all membrane, anode, and electrolyte details.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Mediation","def":"A pathway through which a treatment affects an outcome. Architecture changing CO access is a possible mediator; predictive association alone does not establish mediation.","src":"P04"},{"term":"N₂","def":"Molecular nitrogen, used as the comparison activation atmosphere in the pilot. Subsequent performance testing still uses CO.","src":"P03"},{"term":"n-Propanol","def":"The straight-chain three-carbon alcohol, CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH. It is the target product in the primary response, not the only product generated.","src":"P05"},{"term":"NMR","def":"Nuclear magnetic resonance. Quantitative NMR is part of the proposed liquid-product analysis. Calibration, recovery, processing, and instrument availability determine usable data.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Operando","def":"A measurement made while the material operates under relevant reaction conditions. Optional beamtime may strengthen selected interpretations but is not guaranteed.","src":"P10"},{"term":"Partial current density","def":"The magnitude of current per electrode area attributed to making a specified product. For one product, |j_total| times its Faradaic efficiency.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Phase fraction","def":"The measured fraction associated with a material phase, such as an oxide phase. It is not fixed simply because the starting reagent recipe is the same.","src":"P04"},{"term":"Policy","def":"The decision strategy determining which experiment or measurement to perform next. Policies must be compared under matched opportunities and resource accounting.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Posterior","def":"The model’s updated distribution after the data acquired so far. It expresses uncertainty conditional on the model assumptions, not guaranteed truth.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Precursor","def":"The material prepared before activation. Copper nitrate is the coating ingredient; the heated copper-oxide material is the precursor whose retention is studied.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Precision","def":"How narrow an uncertainty statement is. High precision does not imply low error or good calibration.","src":"P02"},{"term":"Preparation holdout","def":"An independently made specimen or preparation batch withheld while its condition may already be known. It tests reproducibility on fresh material.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Prospective campaign","def":"Actual new experiments executed after defining the policy and evaluation rules, unlike replaying an existing archive.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Recovery","def":"The fraction of produced material successfully accounted for by collection and analysis. Apparent low product output can result from missed product rather than chemistry.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Reduction endpoint","def":"The actual material state after the imposed activation history. Equal charge passed does not prove equal oxide conversion.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Replay","def":"Sequentially revealing already recorded data only when a simulated policy selects and pays for its action. Limited to the specimens and measurements in the archive.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Replication","def":"An independent repeat at the level needed for the question. New coating/heating preparations test a processing claim; repeated readings test analytical repeatability.","src":"P03"},{"term":"Resource cost","def":"The declared combination of preparation, instrument occupation, analysis, and delay. The actual weights must be set and their influence reported.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Scheduler","def":"Software translating chosen actions into ordered, executable instrument jobs and handling delayed results and resource conflicts.","src":"P08"},{"term":"Selectivity","def":"How strongly the reaction favors a product under stated conditions. A product ratio is one descriptor but can improve while absolute production falls.","src":"P05"},{"term":"Space-filling design","def":"A fixed experimental plan spread across the allowed input region. It is a baseline, not a guarantee that the material response itself is uniform.","src":"P09"},{"term":"TEM","def":"Transmission electron microscopy. Selected blinded measurements investigate surface and grain/subgrain structure; preparation, sampling, and companion limitations remain.","src":"P04"},{"term":"θ(t)","def":"The activation effect at dwell t minus the activation effect at the reference dwell. It is the common scientific target for acquisition and scoring.","src":"P07"},{"term":"Transfer","def":"Prediction or interpretation extending to an unobserved condition. It is distinct from reproducing the same recipe in a new preparation.","src":"P09"},{"term":"Wetting","def":"How liquid contacts and accesses the electrode structure. Changes can be an alternative pathway or part of how architecture affects performance.","src":"P04"},{"term":"XRD / diffraction","def":"A structural probe based on scattering from ordered material. The proposal uses it for phases and coherent-domain dimensions, not as a unique map of all surface sites.","src":"P04"}];
